  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-12">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
  <div class="space-y-6 text-body dark:text-muted">
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How much MPF is tax deductible in Hong Kong?</h3>
      <p>Your standard mandatory employee MPF contributions are completely deductible up to HK$18,000 per year. On top of that foundation, if you intentionally open a special Tax Deductible Voluntary Contributions (TVC) account, you can claim an additional, massive deduction of up to HK$60,000 per year.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is MPF deducted directly from my salary tax?</h3>
      <p>Yes, MPF contributions directly reduce your assessable income for salaries tax purposes. This effectively and legally lowers your final tax liability, especially if you are taxed at the higher progressive rates. You can accurately model this exact financial impact using our <a href="/countries/hong-kong/salaries-tax/salaries-tax-calculator/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">HK salaries tax calculator</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the functional difference between MPF and ORSO?</h3>
      <p>MPF is the modern, mandatory, trust-based retirement scheme covering the vast majority of Hong Kong employees today. ORSO refers to older, pre-existing occupational retirement schemes established before 2000. While the MPF system has highly standardized tax deduction rules (like the strict HK$18k cap), ORSO schemes can have vastly different operational, withdrawal, and vesting rules, though they generally share the exact same HK$18,000 tax deduction cap for individuals.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Can I claim MPF tax deductions if I am self-employed?</h3>
      <p>Absolutely. Self-employed persons are legally mandated to join an MPF scheme and make mandatory contributions based purely on their assessable profits. These mandatory contributions (up to HK$18,000) can be directly deducted from your profits tax. For a much deeper dive into this, check out our comprehensive <a href="/blogs/hong-kong-profits-tax-guide/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">Hong Kong profits tax guide</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Do I need to report MPF deductions myself on my annual tax return?</h3>
      <p>For standard employees, your employer will automatically report your mandatory MPF contributions on the annual Employer’s Return (Form BIR56B), and the IRD usually populates this data automatically. However, you must always diligently double-check the figures on your own tax return (Form BIR60) to avoid nasty surprises. Crucially, if you are claiming TVC deductions, you must fill out the relevant section on your tax return manually yourself.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What happens if I over-contribute to my MPF account?</h3>
      <p>If you naively make voluntary contributions beyond the mandatory 5% to a regular account, they are simply not tax-deductible. If you over-contribute to a TVC account far beyond the HK$60,000 cap, you will only legally receive the deduction up to the strict HK$60,000 limit. The excess amount will simply grow in your MPF account without generating any direct, immediate tax benefit.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Can I legally use my MPF to pay my taxes?</h3>
      <p>No, your MPF funds are locked in a heavily regulated trust for your retirement and cannot be withdrawn early to pay your tax bill or resolve any other personal debts. You can only access the funds early under extremely specific legal conditions, such as early retirement at age 60, permanent departure from Hong Kong, or total incapacity/terminal illness.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

